          {
            "citation_index": 5,
            "publication_id": "Interplay of space radiation and microgravity in DNA damage and DNA damage response.",
            "title": "Interplay of space radiation and microgravity in DNA damage and DNA damage response.",
            "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5460239/",
            "doi": "10.1038/s41526-017-0019-7",
            "journal": "NPJ Microgravity",
            "passage": "imulated microgravity has been reported to induce DNA single strand breaks in human retinal pigment epithelial cells (hRPE) cultured in RWV. At 48 h after returning to the 1 g gravity condition, this DNA damage persisted and the production of the inflammatory marker prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) increased. However, hRPE cells previously treated with the anti-inflammatory agent cysteine showed less DNA damage and no PGE2 release. 13\nThe bed rest model for studying the effect of microgravity on human physiology has also revealed an increase of 8-oxo-7,8 dihydro-2′ deoxyguanosine (8-oxo-HdG), which is considered a marker of oxidative DNA damage.",
            "section": "unknown",
            "page": null,
            "relevance": 0.015873,
            "chunk_id": "Interplay_of_space_radiation_a_11060_316a7b",
