      "answer": {
        "answer": "O corpus recuperado menciona que dezenas de *orphan genes* (genes órfãos) apresentam expressão diferencial quando submetidos à irradiação espacial combinada com o tratamento com antagomirs [1]. Além disso, o estudo em *Arabidopsis thaliana* aponta a presença de genes relacionados a danos no DNA causados por radiação e outros estressores no voo espacial, destacando genes ATR/ATM que estão ligados a reguladores do desenvolvimento de raízes e do crescimento vegetal [2]. \n\nAs passagens também indicam que a adaptação de plantas ao ambiente espacial levou à descoberta de um gene previamente não caracterizado denominado *OMG1*, pertencente à família de proteínas *CONSTANS-Like*, que é induzido no espaço e participa da manutenção da via de espécies reativas de oxigênio (ROS) [5]. No entanto, as passagens fornecidas não esgotam a identificação completa de todos os genes específicos que respondem à radiação espacial além desses grupos citados.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "citation_index": 1,
            "publication_id": "Space radiation damage rescued by inhibition of key spaceflight associated miRNAs",
            "title": "Space radiation damage rescued by inhibition of key spaceflight associated miRNAs",
            "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11166944/",
            "doi": "10.1038/s41467-024-48920-y",
            "journal": "Nat Commun",
            "passage": "n orphan genes respond to space radiation and antagomir treatment, differential expression of orphan genes was assessed in the RNA-seq data. In both models, tens of orphan genes were differentially expressed by space irradiation plus antagomirs, but not with radiation alone (Fig. 4c, d\n, and Supplementary Fig. 4\n). Since orphan genes often integrate themselves into metabolic and regulatory networks\n31\n,\n33\n,\n34\n, these could play a role in the dysregulation of DNA repair and metabolic pathways associated with response to space radiation and antagomir treatment observed here.",
            "section": "unknown",
            "page": null,
            "relevance": 0.032787,
            "chunk_id": "Space_radiation_damage_rescued_19749_6e500c",
            "channels": [
              "semantic",
              "lexical"
            ],
            "cited": true
          },
          {
            "citation_index": 2,
            "publication_id": "Detection of Genes in Arabidopsis thaliana L. Responding to DNA Damage from Radiation and Other Stressors in Spaceflight",
            "title": "Detection of Genes in Arabidopsis thaliana L. Responding to DNA Damage from Radiation and Other Stressors in Spaceflight",
            "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8234954/",
            "doi": "10.3390/genes12060938",
            "journal": "Genes (Basel)",
            "passage": "non-radiation-induced genes that respond to other environmental stressors such as ROS, salt, light, heat, and water changes in spaceflight. The MB causal relational subnetworks show the ATR/ATM genes related with genes that regulate root development, and plant growth, indicating that increased radiation tolerance by genetic activations may have a side effect on the plant growth. This fact has to be considered while genetically engineering plants for DNA repair response which may have undesired side-effects such as reduced yield.",
